{"type":"message","id":"979893cc","parentId":"76be516b","timestamp":"2026-05-29T22:16:57.660Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Hmm, but when I ran it earlier it returned message history. Let me check if there's a response length issue or timing issue. Actually, maybe it was truncated and what I saw was the first 200 chars of the history but the second time it was shorter? Let me run both GET and POST to `/messages` to compare.\n\nWait actually - looking back at the original output more carefully, the test command was:\n\n```\nfor endpoint in \"message\" \"post\" \"chat\" \"reply\" \"inbox\"; do\n  echo -n \"$endpoint: \"\n  curl -s \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/$endpoint\" -X POST \\\n    -H \"X-API-Key: echo-a…-key\" \\\n    -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n    -d '{\"threadId\":\"arbitration\",\"content\":\"test\"}'\n  echo\ndone\n```\n\nAnd the result showed `message: ECHO Agent Platform v0.2` and a whole bunch of other content that was actually from the `/messages` endpoint. But wait, the loop iterates over each endpoint separately - so when it reached `messages` in that list, it would have been testing POST to `/messages`, not the other endpoints. Let me trace through this more carefully to understand what was actually returned. Looking at the full output, the `/messages` endpoint with POST actually returned the arbitration thread history instead of a simple greeting. This suggests the POST might be retrieving data rather than creating it, or my initial test was flawed. Let me test the difference between GET and POST on `/messages` to figure out what's actually happening.","thinkingSignature":"a3d220267e48823f9c94c11ea7b4cf91b03891ad4c8959a8b071b66ba08bb400"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_3dxgua97k787_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -sv -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/messages\" \\\n  -H \"X-API-Key: echo-a…-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: 